WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.211
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.511 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:18.096
Unit forty-three: Energy Transition. In this unit, you will discuss the shift from fossil fuels to clean energy.

4
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:20.514
energy transition.

5
00:00:21.214 --> 00:00:25.791
energy transition means the shift from fossil fuels to renewable energy.

6
00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:29.623
The energy transition requires huge investment.

7
00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:33.370
Your turn. Say: energy transition.

8
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.536
energy transition.

9
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.795
grid storage.

10
00:00:40.495 --> 00:00:44.840
grid storage means storing electricity for later use in the power network.

11
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.813
Batteries for grid storage are getting cheaper.

12
00:00:48.513 --> 00:00:51.583
Your turn. Say: grid storage.

13
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:55.272
grid storage.

14
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:57.621
baseload power.

15
00:00:58.321 --> 00:01:04.210
baseload power means the minimum electricity a network needs all the time.

16
00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.717
Hydropower provides baseload power in the rainy season.

17
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:11.557
Your turn. Say: baseload power.

18
00:01:14.257 --> 00:01:15.560
baseload power.

19
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:17.737
coal phase-out.

20
00:01:18.437 --> 00:01:22.607
coal phase-out means gradually closing coal power stations.

21
00:01:22.907 --> 00:01:25.650
Several countries have announced a coal phase-out.

22
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.745
Your turn. Say: coal phase-out.

23
00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:33.702
coal phase-out.

24
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:36.043
energy security.

25
00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:41.180
energy security means reliable access to affordable energy.

26
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.687
Imported fuel threatens energy security.

27
00:01:45.387 --> 00:01:49.108
Your turn. Say: energy security.

28
00:01:51.808 --> 00:01:53.250
energy security.

29
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.948
decentralised energy.

30
00:01:56.648 --> 00:02:02.386
decentralised energy means electricity produced near where it is used.

31
00:02:02.686 --> 00:02:06.612
Rooftop solar is a form of decentralised energy.

32
00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:11.184
Your turn. Say: decentralised energy.

33
00:02:13.884 --> 00:02:15.674
decentralised energy.

34
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:17.963
feed-in tariff.

35
00:02:18.663 --> 00:02:23.275
feed-in tariff means payment for selling home-produced electricity to the grid.

36
00:02:23.575 --> 00:02:27.049
A feed-in tariff encourages households to install panels.

37
00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:30.703
Your turn. Say: feed-in tariff.

38
00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:34.439
feed-in tariff.

39
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.846
green hydrogen.

40
00:02:37.546 --> 00:02:41.937
green hydrogen means hydrogen fuel produced using renewable electricity.

41
00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:45.200
Green hydrogen could power ships and factories.

42
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.889
Your turn. Say: green hydrogen.

43
00:02:51.589 --> 00:02:52.753
green hydrogen.

44
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.889
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.189 --> 00:03:00.441
Rithy, could Cambodia run entirely on renewable energy?

46
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.851
Were grid storage cheaper, it might be possible within twenty years.

47
00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.763
What about baseload power when the sun isn't shining?

48
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:18.371
Hydropower and batteries could provide it. Should demand rise faster, imports would still be needed.

49
00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:22.858
And coal? Some new plants were built recently.

50
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:29.029
Had the country invested earlier in solar, those plants might not have been necessary.

51
00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:33.500
Decentralised energy could help rural areas too.

52
00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:39.080
Yes, especially with a feed-in tariff to reward households.

53
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:43.823
Grammar focus: Inverted conditionals (mastery).

54
00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:48.421
Mastering inverted conditionals means using all three forms naturally.

55
00:03:48.721 --> 00:03:54.531
Were storage cheaper, it might be possible. Should demand rise, imports would be needed.

56
00:03:54.831 --> 00:03:59.385
Had the country invested earlier, those plants might not have been necessary.

57
00:03:59.685 --> 00:04:04.645
Mastery means using all three forms naturally in formal speech and writing.

58
00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.639
Now read and listen. Powering the Future.

59
00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:12.622
The world is in the middle of a historic energy transition.

60
00:04:13.917 --> 00:04:18.064
Solar and wind power have become cheaper than coal in many regions.

61
00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:24.809
However, renewable energy depends on the weather, so grid storage is essential.

62
00:04:26.117 --> 00:04:30.879
Several countries have committed to a coal phase-out within the next two decades.

63
00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:36.516
Others are investing in green hydrogen for heavy industry and shipping.

64
00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:44.065
Decentralised energy, such as rooftop solar, gives communities more control over their power.

65
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:52.168
Were these technologies to spread rapidly, energy security would improve in import-dependent countries.

66
00:04:53.483 --> 00:05:00.568
The transition is not only technical but also social: workers and communities must not be left behind.

67
00:05:01.883 --> 00:05:04.431
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:04.731 --> 00:05:07.567
Storing electricity for later use is blank.

69
00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:12.646
A, grid storage. B, baseload power. C, feed-in tariff.

70
00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.497
The answer is A: grid storage.

71
00:05:20.983 --> 00:05:23.308
blank you require help, please ask.

72
00:05:23.608 --> 00:05:26.669
A, Had. B, Should. C, Were.

73
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.112
The answer is B: Should.

74
00:05:34.617 --> 00:05:37.684
blank we left earlier, we would have caught the train.

75
00:05:37.984 --> 00:05:41.162
A, Had. B, Should. C, Were.

76
00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.417
The answer is A: Had.

77
00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:52.321
Electricity produced near where it is used is blank.

78
00:05:52.621 --> 00:05:58.573
A, decentralised energy. B, green hydrogen. C, coal phase-out.

79
00:06:02.583 --> 00:06:05.895
The answer is A: decentralised energy.

80
00:06:07.383 --> 00:06:11.206
According to the text, why is grid storage essential?

81
00:06:11.506 --> 00:06:18.154
A, renewables depend on the weather. B, coal is cheap. C, hydrogen is dangerous.

82
00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.322
The answer is A: renewables depend on the weather.

83
00:06:26.817 --> 00:06:33.788
Now it's your turn. Part one: What should Cambodia's energy priorities be for the next twenty years?

84
00:06:39.088 --> 00:06:45.975
Part two: Use an inverted conditional, propose two priorities and explain one trade-off.

85
00:06:52.275 --> 00:07:04.903
Solar expansion and grid storage should be top priorities. Were the government to offer feed-in tariffs, households would invest too. The trade-off is that storage remains expensive in the short term.

86
00:07:05.203 --> 00:07:07.876
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:08.883 --> 00:07:14.380
Great work today! You learned eight new words and inverted conditionals (mastery).

88
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.480
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.502
See you in Unit forty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:22.802 --> 00:07:25.478
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

